1) Chief complaint & onset: dysphagia (1 month), psychosis/cognitive decline (1 year);  
2) Course: progressive, with new bulbar and motor features;  
3) Associated symptoms: involuntary movements, dysarthria, irritability, hallucinations, memory loss;  
4) Past history: depression, paternal suicide (possible HD prodrome);  
5) Medications/allergies: none reported;  
6) Social/exposures: cocaine use (not explanatory), no toxins/infections;  
7) Physical exam surrogate: fidgetiness/involuntary movements confirmed;  
8) Imaging: diagnostic caudate atrophy.  

No further information is needed to identify the single most likely diagnosis.

[FINISH]

